---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';

const siteUrl = SITE.url.replace(/\/$/, '');

const description =
  'Where CertKit, SSL Shopper and certkit.io process your private key, how to check it yourself in DevTools, and the honest limits of browser-local tools.';

const pageLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'WebPage',
  name: 'Private keys never leave your browser',
  url: `${siteUrl}/private-keys/`,
  description,
  isPartOf: { '@type': 'WebSite', name: SITE.name, url: `${siteUrl}/` },
};
---

<Base title="Private keys never leave your browser" description={description} jsonLd={[pageLd]}>
  <div class="wrap tool-wrap">
    <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <ol>
        <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
        <li aria-current="page">Private keys</li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <header class="tool-head">
      <h1>Private keys never leave your browser</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        CertKit reads your <span class="mono">.pfx</span>, PEM or DER file in the tab you are
        looking at. The key is not uploaded, because there is no server step in the tool. Below:
        what that means, how other converters handle the same file, and how to confirm it yourself
        in about a minute.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="section-tight" aria-labelledby="client-side">
      <h2 id="client-side">What “client-side” means in practice</h2>
      <div class="card">
        <p>
          When you choose a file or paste a key, the browser hands those bytes to JavaScript running
          in this tab. That is the whole pipeline:
        </p>
        <ol class="steps">
          <li>
            The file is read with the browser’s File API. It stays in this tab’s memory; no form is
            posted.
          </li>
          <li>
            A local copy of <span class="mono">node-forge 1.4.0</span>, served from this domain,
            parses and decrypts the container.
          </li>
          <li>
            The result is written into the page: textareas you can copy, and downloads built as
            in-memory blobs.
          </li>
          <li>
            No request carries the file, the password or the key. The only network calls the tools
            make are for their own code, plus the optional “Load sample” button, which fetches a
            throwaway fixture from this site.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          “Server-side” is the other arrangement: your browser posts the file to a web server, that
          server decrypts it, and the key exists for a moment in a process you do not control. It
          works, and plenty of tools do it, but it is a different trust decision. A server-side
          converter can also log request bodies, keep temporary files or sit behind a proxy you
          have never heard of. CertKit removes that decision from the list.
        </p>
        <p class="small muted">
          You do not have to take our word for any of this: the site source, the vendored library
          and the browser tests are public in the
          <a href="https://github.com/pantha704/certkit-mini-saas">repository</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-tight" aria-labelledby="comparison">
      <h2 id="comparison">Where each converter processes your key</h2>
      <p>
        Every cell below is taken from the vendor’s own page, fetched on 22 October 2026. Nothing
        here says another tool is malicious. It says where the key is processed, which is a
        question you can answer for yourself.
      </p>

      <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Key handling comparison table">
        <table class="data">
          <caption>Key handling compared, pages checked 22 October 2026</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Tool</th>
              <th scope="col">Where the key is processed</th>
              <th scope="col">Upload required</th>
              <th scope="col">Account required</th>
              <th scope="col">Price</th>
              <th scope="col">Source (checked)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">CertKit (this site)</th>
              <td>
                In your browser tab. No request carries the file; the parser runs from scripts served
                by this domain.
              </td>
              <td>No</td>
              <td>No</td>
              <td>Free</td>
              <td>
                <a href="https://github.com/pantha704/certkit-mini-saas">Site source</a> plus the
                DevTools check below · 22 Oct 2026
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">SSL Shopper SSL Converter</th>
              <td>
                On their server. The form is
                <span class="mono">method="post" enctype="multipart/form-data"</span> to
                <span class="mono">/assets/snippets/sslshopper/ajax/ajax_convert.php</span>, with
                <span class="mono">keyFile</span> and <span class="mono">pfxPassword</span> fields.
                Their own note says the key “is sent to the server”.
              </td>
              <td>Yes</td>
              <td>No</td>
              <td>Free, ad-supported</td>
              <td>
                <a href="https://www.sslshopper.com/ssl-converter.html"
                  >sslshopper.com/ssl-converter.html</a
                > · 22 Oct 2026
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">certkit.io PEM decoder</th>
              <td>
                In the CertKit API, server-side. Their page states: “The contents of the PEM file
                will be sent to the CertKit API for decoding.”
              </td>
              <td>Yes</td>
              <td>No for the free tool; sign-in for the product</td>
              <td>Free tool; product from $99/mo</td>
              <td>
                <a href="https://certkit.io/tools/pem-certificate-decoder"
                  >certkit.io/tools/pem-certificate-decoder</a
                > · 22 Oct 2026
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="small muted">
        Name collision worth knowing: <span class="mono">certkit.io</span> is a commercial
        certificate-lifecycle product from TrackJS LLC, unrelated to this site. Both are called
        CertKit; only one runs in your browser.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section-tight" aria-labelledby="verify">
      <h2 id="verify">How to verify it yourself</h2>
      <p>
        This is a checkable claim, not a trust badge. Do it on a throwaway key the first time, then
        on the tool you actually intend to use.
      </p>
      <div class="card">
        <ol class="steps">
          <li>
            Open <a href="/pfx-to-pem/">PFX → PEM</a> and press <kbd>F12</kbd> (or right-click →
            Inspect) to open DevTools. Switch to the <strong>Network</strong> tab and clear it.
          </li>
          <li>
            Run a conversion, or paste a test PEM into
            <a href="/certificate-decoder/">the certificate decoder</a>.
          </li>
          <li>
            Watch the request list. There is no new request during processing, and no request body
            contains <span class="mono">BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY</span> or the base64 body of your key.
            Use the Network filter box to search for a distinctive string from your file; a
            server-side tool matches, this one does not.
          </li>
          <li>
            The stronger test: load the page, then disconnect from the network (airplane mode, or
            DevTools → Network → Offline). The tools keep working, because the engine is already in
            the tab.
          </li>
          <li>
            Run the same steps on a converter you are comparing against. On SSL Shopper you will see
            the <span class="mono">POST</span> to <span class="mono">ajax_convert.php</span> carrying
            the key file, which is the behaviour their page describes.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p class="small muted">
          Note the one exception: the “Load sample” button does fetch a fixture file from this site.
          It is a test file we ship, never yours.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-tight" aria-labelledby="limits">
      <h2 id="limits">What browser-local does not protect you from</h2>
      <p>
        Client-side processing moves the trust boundary, it does not delete it. These limits are
        real and worth stating plainly.
      </p>
      <div class="card">
        <ul class="limits">
          <li>
            <strong>The page itself is code you have to trust.</strong> If this site were compromised
            or served maliciously, the JavaScript could be changed to upload your key. “Runs
            locally” describes the code that is running, not a guarantee about every future version.
            Mitigations: the site is static files with no server-side code path, deploys come from
            the public repository, and you can read the source before you paste anything sensitive.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Verify the domain.</strong> The address should be
            <span class="mono">localcertkit.pages.dev</span> with a valid certificate. Bookmark it
            rather than following a link from a search result or a message. A lookalike domain can
            serve different JavaScript.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>There is one third-party request on the page.</strong> Cloudflare Web Analytics,
            a cookie-less visit counter, loads from
            <span class="mono">static.cloudflareinsights.com</span>. It receives no file, key or
            password, and it is the only script not served from this domain. It is disclosed in the
            <a href="/privacy/">privacy note</a>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Your browser and machine are part of the boundary.</strong> A malicious extension,
            a compromised browser profile or malware on the device can read anything on the page.
            For production keys, use a clean browser profile on a machine you control, and prefer
            working offline.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>This is not a key-management policy.</strong> Local processing does not decide
            whether a key should exist in a browser at all. Handle production keys on the systems
            that own them, keep backups, and rotate anything that has travelled further than it
            should have.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-tight" aria-labelledby="start">
      <h2 id="start">Start with a throwaway key</h2>
      <p>
        Convert a test file, watch the Network tab, and only then bring a real one. Everything here
        is free and needs no account.
      </p>
      <p class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn" href="/pfx-to-pem/">Convert a PFX to PEM</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/certificate-decoder/">Decode a certificate</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/">All tools</a>
      </p>
      <p class="small muted">
        Related: <a href="/key-cert-match/">key and certificate match</a> ·
        <a href="/privacy/">privacy note</a> · <a href="/roadmap/">roadmap</a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .table-scroll {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin: 1rem 0;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }

  .table-scroll table.data {
    margin: 0;
    min-width: 46rem;
  }

  .table-scroll:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid var(--focus);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  .mono {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .steps {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  .steps li {
    margin-bottom: 0.65rem;
  }

  .limits {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .limits li + li {
    margin-top: 0.85rem;
  }

  .cta-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    align-items: center;
    margin: 1.25rem 0;
  }

  .cta-row .btn {
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  kbd {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.9em;
    border: 1px solid var(--line-strong);
    border-bottom-width: 2px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 0.05rem 0.35rem;
    background: var(--tint);
  }
</style>
